><highlight color="#9643FC" opacity="0.399994" creationdate="D:20250625122727+10'00'" flags="print" IT="HighlightNote" date="D:20250625123043+10'00'" name="db020aca-5ab7-4e45-89ab-6dd38b90edcd" page="11" coords="54.311584,113.243057,101.813461,113.243057,54.311584,104.127487,101.813461,104.127487" rect="54.026718,103.842621,102.098328,113.527924" subject="Comment on Text" title="alexd"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: Lower limb overuse injuries that occur while </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-13.3pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>running, gastrointestinal illnesses and altered cardiac function caused by environmental factors, and respiratory illnesses caused by infection are the most frequently reported health problems in short-course triathletes. This is despite the presence of inconsistent injury and illness definitions and the lack of high-quality </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-13.3pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>prospective studies available. Implementation of best-practice surveillance methods inclusive of entiretraining periods, not just competitions, will provide a thorough under-standing of the injury and illness profiles in short-course triath-letes, enabling the development of prevention programs to optimize health and performance in this population.</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="11" rect="595.276001,21.243057,779.276001,113.243057"
/></highlight
